From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: rockchip: rkvenc: Add the RK3528 VEPU540C H.264 back end

The RK3528 encoder is a VEPU540C: the RKVENC v1 programming model with
its registers regrouped into classes and no indirect L2 window. Add it
as a second back end behind a table of hardware operations, so that the
stateful front end serves both.

The back end codes H.264 from NV12 at one QP per picture, up to
8192x8192; HEVC is not offered on this core. Past 3072 pixels wide the
line cache spills into an extension buffer, which each reference slot
carries; a reference's body follows its header in one buffer, as the
core writes past the header's size. Encoded and decoded back on an
RK3528, H.264 holds 47 dB or better up to 8192x8192, and 16384x8640
fails, as it does under Rockchip's MPP.

Its register image follows Rockchip MPP's H.264 HAL for the block in
fixed QP mode. Built on a host from the same parameters, the two images
match except for the interrupt enables, the hardware watchdog and the
IDR picture id of P slices, none of which reach the stream.

Signed-off-by: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
